tt_type_comp: say so when the preprocessor is missing.

tt_type_comp runs the types file through the system's cpp (CPP_PROGRAM,
found by configure) and, when that file is not there, reads the source as
it is; the comment above the test promises a warning, but none is
printed. On a system where cpp comes only with the C development set, a
user's types file with #include or #define then fails to parse with no
hint why. Print, once: "tt_type_comp: warning: no preprocessor <path> (it
comes with the C development set): types files are read as they are, so
#include and #define in them are not handled". Nothing else changes.

--- a/lib/tt/bin/tt_type_comp/mp_types_table.C	2026-11-20 12:03:13
+++ b/lib/tt/bin/tt_type_comp/mp_types_table.C	2026-11-20 12:03:13
@@ -121,6 +121,16 @@
 	// This may cause trouble, but it\'s the best thing to try.
 	// A warning will be issued if cpp is not around.
 	use_cpp = (-1 != access(OPT_CPP_PATH,X_OK));
+	if (!use_cpp) {
+		static int warned;
+
+		if (!warned++)
+			fprintf(stderr, "tt_type_comp: warning: no preprocessor "
+				"%s (it comes with the C development set): "
+				"types files are read as they are, so #include "
+				"and #define in them are not handled\n",
+				OPT_CPP_PATH);
+	}
 
 	_file_name = path;
 	if ((fd = open(path, O_RDONLY)) == -1) {
